{"name":"Deep River Green","hex":"#2d4243","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"deep","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Extending, reaching or positioned far from a point of reference, especially downwards.\nExtending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively.","Extending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Far, especially far down through something or into something, physically or figuratively.","In a profound, not superficial, manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/deep"},{"term":"river","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large and often winding stream which drains a land mass, carrying water down from higher areas to a lower point, oftentimes ending in another body of water, such as an ocean or in an inland sea.","Any large flow of a liquid in a single body."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To improve one’s hand to beat another player on the final card in a poker game."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/river"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"River","blocks":[{"text":"A river is a natural stream of fresh water that flows on land or inside caves towards another body of water at a lower elevation, such as an ocean, lake, or another river. A river may run dry before reaching the end of its course if it runs out of water, or only flow during certain seasons. Rivers are regulated by the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Water first enters rivers through precipitation, whether from rainfall, the runoff of water down a slope, the melting of glaciers or snow, or seepage from aquifers beneath the surface of the Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg/330px-Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/NorthAmerica-WaterDivides.png/500px-NorthAmerica-WaterDivides.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/NorthAmerica-WaterDivides.png/500px-NorthAmerica-WaterDivides.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The major drainage basins in North America","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANorthAmerica-WaterDivides.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Perito_Moreno_Glacier_2023.jpg/500px-Perito_Moreno_Glacier_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Perito_Moreno_Glacier_2023.jpg/1280px-Perito_Moreno_Glacier_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Perito_Moreno_Glacier_2023.jpg/500px-Perito_Moreno_Glacier_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Melting toe of the Perito Moreno Glacier in Los Glaciares National Park, Argentina","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APerito_Moreno_Glacier_2023.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Grand_Canyon_Powell_Point_Evening_Light_02_2013.jpg/500px-Grand_Canyon_Powell_Point_Evening_Light_02_2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Grand_Canyon_Powell_Point_Evening_Light_02_2013.jpg/1280px-Grand_Canyon_Powell_Point_Evening_Light_02_2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Grand_Canyon_Powell_Point_Evening_Light_02_2013.jpg/500px-Grand_Canyon_Powell_Point_Evening_Light_02_2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Grand Canyon was carved by the Colorado River.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGrand_Canyon_Powell_Point_Evening_Light_02_2013.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d